### Describe the bug

We are currently evalutating the eprformance of zenoh. We want to use zenoh as a real-time messaging layer. We set up a benchmark. The latencies are much higher than those listed on the website.
We also tried the shared memory option, but it didn't make much of a difference. Perhaps we configured something incorrectly?


### To reproduce

1. We spawn two threads, assign them RT prios, and place them on isolated cores.
2. In these threads, we spawn a Tokio runtime.
3. We open a session with
```rust
let mut zenoh_cfg = zenoh::Config::default();
zenoh_cfg.set_mode(Some(zenoh::config::WhatAmI::Peer));
zenoh_cfg.transport.shared_memory.set_enabled(true);
zenoh::open(zenoh_cfg).await.unwrap()
```
4.  We send X samples from one thread to another (No shm). We used CLOCK_REALTIME because we disabled NTP.
```rust
// Thread TX
let publisher = session.declare_publisher("key/expression").await.unwrap();
for i in 0..X{
  // Save timestamp
  tx_times.push((
      i,
      nix::time::clock_gettime(ClockId::CLOCK_REALTIME).unwrap(),
  ));
  buf[0..size_of::<usize>()].copy_from_slice(&i.to_ne_bytes());
  publisher.put::<&[u8]>(buf.as_ref()).await.unwrap();
}

//Thread RX
let subscriber = session.declare_subscriber("key/expression").await.unwrap();
loop {
  // break after all received or timeout
  Ok(sample) = subscriber.recv_async() => {
    let payload = sample.payload().to_bytes();
    let bytes = payload.as_ref();
    let val: usize = usize::from_le_bytes([
      bytes[0], bytes[1], bytes[2], bytes[3], bytes[4], bytes[5], bytes[6],
      bytes[7],
